class.description:
One object carries the joining side and the authority side, because a listen-server host is both at once and a single policy has to speak for it. The joining peer runs [method _prepare] and then [method _credentials] to produce the bytes it presents; the authority runs [method _verify] on those bytes and reaches an [AuthResult]. [method _host_identity] is what the host says about itself. [NetwAuthProtocol] carries those bytes over the wire and decides nothing; this class decides and never touches the wire.
Every seam has a stock answer, and the stock answers are deliberately asymmetric: [method prepare_default] resolves at once, [method credentials_default] presents no bytes, [method host_identity_default] answers [code]null[/code], and [method verify_default] REJECTS. So a flow that is installed but never implements [method _verify] admits nobody, rather than admitting everybody. A GDScript subclass cannot [code]super()[/code] into a virtual, so those four [code]_default[/code] methods are an override's only route back to stock behaviour.
[codeblock]
extends NetwAuthFlow

func _credentials(username: StringName) -> PackedByteArray:
    return _token.to_utf8_buffer()

func _verify(peer_id: int, data: PackedByteArray) -> AuthResult:
    var identity := _lookup(data.get_string_from_utf8())
    if identity == null:
        return AuthResult.reject("unknown token")
    return AuthResult.accept(identity)

[/codeblock]
A game declares one through [method Netw.configure_auth], from a factory that runs once per session on the first authentication that needs it. The session then keeps the flow it built, so the node that declared the factory can go without taking the policy with it. [member NetwMultiplayer.auth_flow] answers which flow this session actually runs, and a session that declared none admits every peer.
[codeblock]
func _init() -> void:
    Netw.configure_auth(self, make_auth)

func make_auth() -> NetwAuthFlow:
    return MyAuth.new()
[/codeblock]
[method _prepare] answers a [NetwPromise], and a configured flow that prepares nothing, or rejects, refuses the join rather than releasing it as prepared. A [method AuthResult.reject] is the answer the game was asked for and is reported at debug level only; only a flow that answers no [AuthResult] at all is a fault.
